      "math_academy_id": "q-41787",
      "knowledge_point_id": "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/7a3c82568cfaf845708a9dcee79a3ac2e0e9d2131776fd1c7daa1c598473f3b3.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=10$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(10,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-5\\sqrt{3},-5).$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "(-5,-5)"
            },
