    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-87135",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d90b9572-cdac-51f7-b386-81b750642585",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line That Passes Through a Given Point",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/5507850932bb4061cd19cdbffa18aacf2b235293e643170ee78c4326a96e050d.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(9,-3\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{9}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$",
